---
// Lesson 8.1 — Boss Design: Reuse & Brainstorm
// Two weeks between Thanksgiving and Winter Break: students build the Boss Fight
// independently by reusing elements they already built (`EnemyController`, `TurretController`,
// `Projectile`, `PlayerProjectile`, `Platform`, `Spawner`, `StatManager`, `Lever`, `SceneSwitch`)
// and following Brainstorm -> Prototype -> Self-Playtest -> Friend Playtest -> Revise.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>8.1 — Boss Design: Reuse &amp; Brainstorm</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Between Thanksgiving and Winter Break you are building the Boss Fight on your own. There is
    no pre-built Boss prefab in the project — instead, you already have every building block inside
    <code>Assets/Scripts/</code>. Today you brainstorm multiple boss concepts by remixing existing
    parts, pick one you can actually finish in two weeks, and sketch it before opening Unity.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Reuse Catalog: What You Already Built</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Before inventing scripts from scratch, inventory the working prefabs and C# classes already
        inside your repository. A great Unity boss is often two or three familiar pieces combined
        in a dramatic room:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Existing Piece</th><th>What It Already Does</th><th>How to Reuse It on a Boss</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>EnemyController</code></td>
            <td>Walks <code>patrolPoints</code>, tracks <code>target</code> in range, has <code>health</code>, <code>ChangeHealth()</code>, and drops <code>itemDrop</code> inside <code>Die()</code>.</td>
            <td>Scale it up into the main boss body, speed up patrol phases, or drop the victory key/portal prefab on <code>Die()</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>TurretController</code> + <code>Projectile</code></td>
            <td>Fires <code>fireballPrefab</code> tagged <code>E_Fireball</code> every <code>timeBetweenShots</code> seconds using <code>Shoot()</code>.</td>
            <td>Mount turrets as boss weak points, corner cannons, or flying orb phases that track the player automatically.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Platform</code></td>
            <td>Moves along <code>wayPoints</code> via <code>MoveTowards()</code> and parents the player via <code>SetParent()</code>.</td>
            <td>Build moving boss battlegrounds, elevator shields blocking shots, or a rising escape platform when defeated.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Spawner</code></td>
            <td>Calls <code>Instantiate()</code> to drop <code>prefabToSpawn</code> into the scene.</td>
            <td>Convert the trigger into a timed wave or phase-2 minion spawner inside the arena.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Lever</code> + <code>SceneSwitch</code></td>
            <td>Fires <code>onLeverOn</code> UnityEvents or moves the player to the next scene cleanly.</td>
            <td>Lower a boss barrier, trigger hazard lights, or warp back to a victory screen once the boss drops the exit.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>StatManager</code></td>
            <td>Holds <code>health</code>, <code>coins</code>, HUD text, and <code>jumpsRemaining</code> across every scene; <code>Die()</code> reloads the arena cleanly.</td>
            <td>Tracks survival through the boss room and resets the entire arena cleanly if the player runs out of health.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Remixing Before Writing</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Look at the table above: which three existing components can create a multi-phase boss fight before you write a single new script?</li>
            <li><code>PlayerController.Update()</code> switches gravity on automatically via <code>Start()</code> in every scene except <code>Overworld</code>. If you put the boss in its own <code>Boss</code> scene, what does the player get for free without editing scene detection?</li>
            <li>In <code>EnemyController</code>, destroying a boss on <code>Die()</code> already instantiates <code>itemDrop</code>. Why does dropping a reward, portal, or <code>SceneSwitch</code> object beat teleporting silently the millisecond <code>health &lt;= 0</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor: Brainstorm &amp; Spec on Paper (~35 min)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — Divergent brainstorm (10 min):</strong> Sketch <strong>three completely different</strong>
        boss concepts in three boxes. Do not pick your first idea immediately — push yourself to
        try one stationary multi-turret machine, one mobile patrolling stalker, and one arena-puzzle
        boss (platforms + levers + hazards).
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — Pick one and write the build spec (25 min):</strong> Choose the idea that
        has the clearest silhouette and can reach a playable prototype in your very next class:
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li><strong>Arena layout:</strong> draw a side-view map showing floors tagged <code>Ground</code>, any ladders or moving <code>Platform</code> lifts, turret mounts, and player safe spots.</li>
        <li><strong>Player verbs:</strong> say how BOTH <code>KeyCode.E</code> melee (<code>attackBox</code>) and <code>KeyCode.Space</code> ranged (<code>PlayerProjectile</code>) interact with the boss, plus jumping and double jumping.</li>
        <li><strong>Two phases:</strong> describe Phase 1 (calm, teaches the tell) and Phase 2 (under 50% health — faster timer, second turret, moving lift turns on, or minions appear).</li>
        <li><strong>Win &amp; Lose loop:</strong> what spawns or opens when the boss hits zero health, and how does <code>StatManager.Die()</code> reset everything cleanly if the player loses?</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Two-week scope rule:</strong> pick ONE boss room, ONE clear health bar or visual
        damage tell, and AT MOST TWO distinct attacks or phases. Over-ambitious cutscenes and six
        untested phases leave nothing playable for friend playtesting next week.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Defending the Design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>From your three brainstorms, why did you reject the other two — and which single mechanic from your chosen idea is riskiest to build first?</li>
            <li>Sketch the attack tell: what does the player <em>see or hear</em> half a second before damage happens so getting hit feels fair rather than random?</li>
            <li>Where in this fight do both <code>KeyCode.E</code> melee and <code>KeyCode.Space</code> fireballs matter (or why does one stay risky while the other stays slow)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Brainstorm three distinct boss concepts that reuse existing game scripts and prefabs</li>
        <li>Select one realistic two-week scope and sketch the arena, platforms, and hazard placements</li>
        <li>Specify how the player damages the boss, how the boss escalates in Phase 2, and how victory triggers</li>
        <li>Commit and push your spec notes before opening prototyping</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/index.html">Unit 8: Boss Fight</a> — independent boss design &amp; playtest plan</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/enemy-state-machines.html">Enemy State Machines</a> — how <code>EnemyController</code> patrols and chases</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — colliders, tags, triggers, and <code>Instantiate()</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="8.2.html">8.2 — Boss Prototype I: Arena, Body &amp; Health</a> — get a playable prototype on screen fast.</p>
</div>`;
